{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"alias":"unplayful-inaccurate-muskrat","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context and evidence\n\nThe remaining macOS critical path is filesystem-heavy. The N=3000 import long pole reproduces at 20.19-21.63s on the same macOS host with 15.39-16.65s system CPU versus 4.59-5.67s user CPU. cProfile attributes 13.69s to 24 required git-add/git-commit pairs, while the full default suite creates thousands of temporary Git stores below pytest --basetemp. The workflow deliberately uses runner.temp rather than /tmp because the macOS tmp cleaner previously deleted live test trees.\n\nMaintained OSS precedent matches this mechanism. Apple Git CI creates a 4GB APFS RAM disk for test output and documents greater-than-2x test speedup from avoiding slow storage for many small writes (apple-oss-distributions/Git src/git/.gitlab-ci.yml lines 82-88 at 6b2f9bfe). Aeron, an active high-throughput OSS project, creates a macOS APFS RAM disk in GitHub Actions for its temporary test filesystem (aeron-io/aeron .github/workflows/ci.yml lines 62-68 at 092f184a). Git t/perf/README also says putting test roots on tmpfs can massively speed the suite. Tracker search found no prior RAM-disk/tmpfs proposal or rejection. This changes storage placement only: no test, worker, platform, behavioral assertion, coverage, mutation work, or runner is removed or added.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. Add always-run, non-secret telemetry around the existing default and integration basetemps: allocated bytes, file count, filesystem type, and available capacity. Preserve the existing runner.temp paths for an initial Gerrit patchset and use its completed macOS job as the sizing/control observation.\n2. Size the RAM disk from observed peak retained temp bytes with at least 50% headroom, while keeping it below 40% of runner physical memory. Record the calculation on this ticket. If the observed requirement cannot fit within that bound, stop and report the remediation as infeasible rather than risking memory pressure or a full volume.\n3. In the existing build/test matrix job, create an APFS RAM disk only when runner.os is macOS, after setup/gates and immediately before pytest. Publish its mount path through a step output; non-macOS cells continue using runner.temp byte-for-byte. Route both unchanged pytest selectors to separate basetemps below that mount.\n4. Always detach the exact device created by the setup step after both pytest tiers, including failure/cancellation paths where GitHub permits the cleanup step to run. Emit final used/capacity telemetry before detach. Do not use a broad disk target, persistent mount, cache, extra runner, or extra job.\n5. Add static workflow contract tests proving macOS-only creation, measured bounded sizing, unchanged selectors/xdist worker counts/timeouts/coverage flags, distinct default/integration basetemps, and always-run exact-device cleanup. Run actionlint and zizmor.\n6. Push through Gerrit immediately. Compare the RAM-disk patchset macOS default/integration/job wall times with its control patchset and at least one recent post-DNS green run; report absolute and percentage savings plus Ubuntu non-regression. Retain the change only if the full matrix is green and macOS runner time decreases beyond ordinary run-to-run variation.\n\n## Test contract card\n\nauthoritative_contract: .github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml executes the unchanged default and integration pytest selectors on Ubuntu 3.11/3.12/3.13 and macOS 3.13, with coverage once on Ubuntu 3.13.\ntrigger_preconditions: macOS matrix cell reaches the pytest steps after all existing gates.\nproduction_path: GitHub Actions workflow creates an ephemeral APFS RAM device, pytest tmp_path/basetemp writes real Git repositories there, then cleanup detaches only that device.\ntest_tier: static workflow contract tests plus the actual full Gerrit Verify matrix.\nobservable_postcondition: identical collection/pass/skip counts and gates, lower macOS pytest/job wall time, bounded RAM use, no mounted device after cleanup.\nnegative_control: every Ubuntu cell keeps runner.temp and existing behavior; a RAM-disk setup/sizing failure fails loudly before tests rather than silently changing coverage.\ncollateral_invariants: no test selection, assertion, marker, worker count, timeout, coverage, mutation shard, Python/platform cell, or runner concurrency changes.\nci_gate: actionlint, zizmor, workflow contract tests, full Gerrit Verify, LLM-Review.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A control patchset records macOS basetemp allocated bytes, file count, filesystem type, capacity, and default/integration/job wall times without changing storage placement.\n- [ ] The recorded peak size yields at least 50% RAM-disk headroom and remains below 40% of detected physical memory.\n- [ ] Only the macOS cell creates and uses the APFS RAM disk; all Ubuntu cells retain runner.temp.\n- [ ] Default and integration selectors, markers, xdist counts/distribution, timeouts, coverage-once behavior, collection/pass/skip counts, and all platform/Python cells remain unchanged.\n- [ ] Cleanup runs under always(), reports final usage, and detaches only the exact device created by setup.\n- [ ] Static workflow tests, actionlint, zizmor, focused/full pytest, LLM-Review, and Gerrit Verified are green.\n- [ ] Control-versus-RAM-disk evidence shows a macOS runner-time decrease beyond recent run variation with no Ubuntu regression; otherwise the experimental change is not retained.","id":"9d44-c0c0-8d4d-4074","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["ci","performance","macos","pytest"],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Use a measured macOS RAM disk for Git-heavy pytest temp trees"},"env_id":"0993f6a8-7a6a-4707-a671-c5bbb2691444","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786759962363224001,"uuid":"49995f90-45db-46e8-bd55-5cfb8de4b812"}